/-!
# Newman (2024): *When Arguments Merge*

A head bears Merge features, and a phrase merging with it checks every unchecked feature it can
and at least one. Features may go unchecked, and they belong to categories: V bears `[·D·]`,
checked by a DP, and `[·X·]`, checked by any phrase, and v bears these with `[·V·]` and `[·wh·]`.
A phrase checking a subset of the features another checks must then merge first when both merge
with one head. Among the operations possible at a step of a derivation, Weak Economy prefers one
checking more features, unless either would bleed the other.

This file formalizes [newman-2024]'s theory of feature-driven Merge and four of its applications:
the complement position of non-DP arguments, the binding pattern of the dative alternation, agent
focus in Mayan subject questions, and the subjects of passive ditransitives. The last three are
fitted to the examples of `Data.Examples.Newman2024`.

## Main definitions

* `Licensed`: a sequence of merges with one head, each checking a feature still unchecked.
* `mergeFeatures`, `Phrase.checks`: the features a category bears and those a phrase checks.
* `configuration`: the binding theory, m-command without reverse c-command.
* `IsDitransitive`, `Binds`: a vP that Merge licenses from a ditransitive's arguments, and binding
  between its objects under a word order.
* `AgentFocus`, `verbForm`, `Predicted`: Voice and T cross-reference one argument, the form of
  the verb that results, and the forms of a clause with given fronted arguments.
* `System.Derivation`, `System.Economical`: a derivation in which every step respects Weak
  Economy.
* `high`, `low`, `Predicts`: the operations at v in a ditransitive whose indirect object is in
  v's complement or in V's, and the subjects such a clause can have.

## Implementation notes

* A phrase's checkable features are read off its category and the features it bears. The first
  specifier merged is outermost, by Paillé's generalization of Richards's tucking in; m-command is
  Barker and Pullum's command relation generated by the maximal projections, and case is Marantz's
  dependent case.
* An operation is possible when it checks a Merge feature of an accessible phrase, and a
  derivation ends only when nothing possible remains, as Preminger's obligatory operations
  require. Weak Economy does not compare two operations when either would bleed the other, as in
  the analysis of Greek wh passives, where wh-movement of the indirect object bleeds clitic
  doubling and clitic doubling checks more features.
* `IsDitransitive` takes every vP `Licensed` admits with the direct object at V and the subject
  at v, a VP specifier on either side. That is seven trees (`isDitransitive_iff`): the book draws
  three, and not the four with the subject as v's complement or VP outermost. The binding
  prediction holds on all seven.
* A language of the Mayan type moves the object to check v's `[·D·]` before the subject merges
  wherever that leaves the subject licensed (`Realizes`), and its `[·wh·]` stays active once
  checked. The agent-focus form is read off a row's gloss line, as is a Greek clitic.
* The last-resort model of Coon, Mateo Pedro and Preminger is the one formalized in
  `Studies/CoonMateoPedroPreminger2014.lean`, with a determinerless object as its Caseless
  object.

